    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Got it. So it's basically a balance of chemistry. If a grower sees signs of manganese deficiency despite the pH being in a decent range, what are some of the other environmental factors or soil complexities that might be interfering with that uptake?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If manganese deficiency is observed despite adequate soil pH levels, other factors may be at play. Soil texture, organic matter content, and cation exchange capacity (CEC) can all influence manganese uptake. High amounts of clay soils or compost may bind manganese too tightly, making it less available for the grapevines. Also, high levels of other cations, such as calcium, magnesium, or potassium, might inhibit manganese absorption due to competitive uptake. Additionally, soil moisture and temperature dictate manganese solubility and plant availability; very dry conditions can reduce the solubility and mobility of manganese, diminishing its uptake by plants. Periodic testing of both nutrient content and soil properties is vital for diagnosing and rectifying this issue.",
